    <div class="mb-8"><div class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">August 5, 2026 · VPS Provider Review · 12 min read</div><h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Netcup VPS Review 2026: German KVM from €5.91/mo</h1><p class="text-xl text-gray-400">DDR5 ECC RAM, NVMe, flatrate traffic, and snapshots on a German KVM at budget prices — with a 12-month contract caveat and ticket-only support.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What Netcup Offers in 2026</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup is a German hosting provider (netcup GmbH, Karlsruhe) that has been quietly building one of the strongest hardware-per-euro VPS lines in Europe. The current G12 generation is a big step up from earlier tiers: every x86 plan ships with DDR5 ECC RAM and NVMe storage, flatrate traffic is included, and KVM gives you full root access.</p><p class="text-gray-300">The lineup is straightforward. The VPS 500 G12 starts at €5.91/mo for 2 vCore, 4 GB DDR5 ECC, and 128 GB NVMe. The range scales to the VPS 8000 G12 at €47.95/mo for 16 vCore, 64 GB RAM, and 2 TB NVMe. Hourly billing is available from €0.010/hr on the entry plan, which is useful if you want to test before committing.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Netcup VPS Pricing and the Hardware Case</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-sm"><thead><tr class="border-b border-gray-700 text-gray-400"><th class="text-left py-3 pr-4">Plan</th><th class="text-left py-3 pr-4">vCore</th><th class="text-left py-3 pr-4">RAM</th><th class="text-left py-3 pr-4">NVMe</th><th class="text-left py-3 pr-4">Monthly</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-white">VPS 500 G12</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-gray-300">2 vCore (x86)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">4 GB DDR5 ECC</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">128 GB</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-orange-400">€5.91</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-white">VPS 1000 G12</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-gray-300">4 vCore (x86)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">8 GB DDR5 ECC</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">256 GB</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-orange-400">€10.37</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-white">VPS 2000 G12</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-gray-300">8 vCore (x86)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">16 GB DDR5 ECC</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">512 GB</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-orange-400">€19.25</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-white">VPS 4000 G12</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-gray-300">12 vCore (x86)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">32 GB DDR5 ECC</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">1024 GB</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-orange-400">€32.41</td></tr><tr><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-white">VPS 8000 G12</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-gray-300">16 vCore (x86)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">64 GB DDR5 ECC</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">2048 GB</td><td class="py-3 pr-4 text-orange-400">€47.95</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="text-gray-400 mt-4">Prices shown include 19% VAT, which matters: non-EU buyers see a lower checkout total, while EU businesses can reclaim the VAT. Hourly billing starts at €0.010/hr on the VPS 500 G12. All plans include flatrate traffic, snapshots, a backup system, and a remote console.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Hardware Quality: DDR5 ECC Is the Differentiator</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The most distinctive thing about Netcup's G12 line is ECC RAM across the board. Error-correcting memory is standard on dedicated servers but rare at budget VPS prices. For databases, ZFS pools, and long-running daemons, ECC reduces the risk of silent memory corruption — a real, if uncommon, failure mode on non-ECC hosts.</p><p class="text-gray-300">NVMe storage is on every plan, and the entry tier connects at up to 2.5 Gbit/s. Local block storage can be attached and expanded when the included allocation runs out. KVM virtualization means you boot standard kernels and can mount custom ISOs through the Server Control Panel.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Flatrate Traffic and the Fair-Use Throttle</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup includes flatrate traffic, which is increasingly rare among budget hosts that meter egress. The trade-off is a fair-use rule: on the entry plan, if average traffic over the last 24 hours exceeds 2 TB, the connection is temporarily throttled to 200 Mbit/s until the average drops. Higher plans have proportionally higher thresholds.</p><p class="text-gray-300">In practice this suits most self-hosting workloads — websites, APIs, Docker hosts, backups — where sustained multi-terabyte daily transfer is the exception, not the rule. If you are running a public file mirror or a heavy media server, read the throttle policy carefully before committing.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Networking: IPv4 + IPv6 Included, Cloud vLAN Optional</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Every VPS can be configured with a public IPv4 address plus a /64 IPv6 subnet at no extra cost. Choosing IPv6-only actually reduces the monthly price, which is a nice touch for IPv6-first deployments. A Cloud vLAN option covers private networking between your servers in the same location.</p><p class="text-gray-300">If you need neither public address, a Cloud vLAN-only configuration is available — useful for internal-only services. Netcup's DNS and reverse-DNS tooling is managed through the same control panel.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Datacenters: Five Locations, Availability Varies</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup now lists Vienna (VIE), Nuremberg (NUE), Amsterdam (AMS), Manassas in the USA (MNZ), and Singapore (SIN) for servers, plus a "No preference Europe" option that lets Netcup choose any EU location for you. The European footprint is solid for GDPR-sensitive workloads; the US and Singapore points cover North America and APAC, though APAC coverage is thinner than providers with many Asian regions.</p><p class="text-gray-300">A practical warning: plan availability differs by location. A given G12 plan may be sold out in one datacenter while available in another, and the ARM64 tier has been repeatedly sold out. Check the product page at checkout and be ready to switch locations.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Snapshots, Backups, and the Control Panel</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The Server Control Panel (SCP) handles deployment, reinstallation, snapshots, and DNS. Snapshots use Copy-On-Write, so creating them is fast and they are space-efficient — ideal for testing changes before applying them to production. A separate backup system is bundled with the VPS line, and local block storage can be expanded.</p><p class="text-gray-300">Keep the usual discipline: a snapshot on the same host is not an offsite backup. For anything irreplaceable, push periodic backups to a different provider or location.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Contract Terms: The 12-Month Catch</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup offers hourly billing (paid monthly in advance, settled hourly on cancellation) and monthly contracts, but several configs — especially the cheaper G12 deals — require a 12-month minimum contract period. The advertised €5.91/mo entry price is often the 12-month invoice config.</p><p class="text-gray-300">This changes the value calculation. A €5.91/mo plan costs about €71 for the year, which is still cheap, but it is not a month-to-month commitment. If you need flexibility, confirm the minimum term on the exact plan and location before ordering, or look at hourly-billed alternatives.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Support and SLA</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Support is ticket-based with no phone line. Netcup's culture is German-first, so English tickets work but may feel terse. Response times are generally reasonable for a budget host, but do not expect white-glove hand-holding.</p><p class="text-gray-300">On availability, vServer tariffs advertise a guaranteed minimum availability of 99.6% on an annual average (root servers list 99.9%), and new orders are covered by a 30-day satisfaction guarantee. That is a meaningful SLA for the price tier.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Netcup vs RackNerd and US Budget Hosts</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RackNerd</a> remains the cleaner baseline for buyers focused on ultra-low annual specials and US datacenter coverage. Netcup wins the comparison when hardware quality (DDR5 ECC), flatrate traffic, EU locations, and snapshot tooling matter more than the lowest possible invoice.</p><p class="text-gray-300">For a European production workload with a 12-month runway, Netcup's VPS 1000 G12 at €10.37/mo delivers more RAM and NVMe per euro than most US-based budget hosts. For a throwaway test box or a month-to-month project, RackNerd's hourly and monthly options are easier to walk away from.</p></div>

    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Our Verdict: 4.4/5 for Self-Managed Users in Europe</h2><p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup is one of the best budget KVM values in Europe in 2026. DDR5 ECC RAM, NVMe, flatrate traffic, snapshots, a backup system, and five locations at entry pricing of €5.91/mo is a genuinely strong hardware package.</p><p class="text-gray-300">The 12-month minimum contract on many configs, fair-use throttle rule, sold-out ARM tier, and ticket-only support keep it below the top tier. Before ordering, confirm the minimum contract period and location availability, and set up independent backups. If those checks fit your operating model, Netcup deserves a place on the shortlist.</p></div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8"><div><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-green-400">Who should choose it?</h2><ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300"><li>✅ Docker hosts and self-hosted services in Europe</li><li>✅ Workloads that benefit from ECC RAM (databases, ZFS)</li><li>✅ Buyers who want flatrate traffic and snapshots</li><li>✅ 12-month commitments with a fixed hardware budget</li><li>✅ GDPR-sensitive projects that want EU datacenters</li></ul></div><div><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-red-400">Who should skip it?</h2><ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300"><li>⚠️ Anyone needing month-to-month flexibility on the cheap plans</li><li>⚠️ Beginners looking for managed administration or phone support</li><li>⚠️ Heavy media servers that sustain multi-TB daily traffic</li><li>⚠️ Teams that need many APAC locations beyond Singapore</li><li>⚠️ Anyone without an independent backup plan</li></ul></div></div>

    <div class="bg-orange-500/10 border border-orange-500/20 rounded-xl p-6"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3">Compare VPS pricing before you commit</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Use our <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd comparison link</a> as a low-cost baseline, then match RAM, region, bandwidth, and contract flexibility against Netcup.</p><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-block bg-green-600 hover:bg-green-500 text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-medium transition">Compare VPS options →</a></div>
